dict(n=24, orig=47, answer='A', type='Principle',
     passage=[('conc', 'Acting is surely the most overrated occupation of all time.'),
              ('counter', 'Although top actors are held in high esteem,'),
              ('prem', 'sometimes people walk into the profession without any training and win critical acclaim.'),
              ('evid', 'In the film <i>Captain Phillips</i>, for example, Barkhad Abdi won several awards for best supporting actor, despite having never acted before in his life – he was chosen because of how he looked and, presumably, because he had some natural ability to act.'),
              ('prem', 'Yet how difficult can something be if natural ability is enough to get by? And in any case, no one should be admired simply for having a natural ability.'),
              ('analogy', 'It is like being congratulated for being tall or having good eyesight.'),
              ('conc', 'Film actors do not, therefore, deserve to be held in such high regard.')],
     stem='Which one of the following illustrates the principle used in the above argument?',
     options={'A': 'We should not praise a five-year-old child who picks up a golf club for the first time and consistently strikes the ball cleanly.',
              'B': 'The music Mozart composed towards the end of his life should be thought of more highly than that which he wrote when very young.',
              'C': 'If we know that a writer has spent a long time drafting and re-drafting their work, then their work is automatically deserving of praise.',
              'D': 'A successful musician who happened to be in the right place at the right time when they were first recognised does not deserve the accolades they receive.',
              'E': 'Someone who has a photographic memory and is employed by the security services does not deserve the high salary they might earn.'},
     right='Striking cleanly on the first go is pure natural ability, so by the principle the child should not be praised for it.',
     why={'B': 'Ranks his later works above his early ones. It doesn’t say a natural gift deserves no praise.',
          'C': 'The reverse direction (effort earns praise), and “automatically” goes beyond the argument.',
          'D': 'About luck (right place, right time), not natural ability.',
          'E': 'About pay for doing a job, not admiration for a gift.'},
     working_title='Working: write the principle first',
     working=f"""<p class="skel">Abdi succeeded on <b>natural ability</b>, with no training {ar('pen')} nobody should be admired just for a natural gift (like being tall) {ar('pen')} actors don’t deserve high regard.<br>
So the principle: <b>a natural ability on its own does not deserve admiration</b>.</p>
<div class="map">
<div><b>A</b><span>First time with a golf club, hits cleanly: natural ability → don’t praise. {tick()} Same principle.</span></div>
<div><b>B</b><span>Later works rated above early ones. A ranking, not “no praise for a gift”. {cross()}</span></div>
<div><b>C</b><span>Hard work → automatically praised. The other direction, and too strong. {cross()}</span></div>
<div><b>D</b><span>Lucky timing → no accolades. Luck, not natural ability. {cross()}</span></div>
<div><b>E</b><span>Gift → no high salary. Pay for a job, not admiration. {cross()}</span></div>
</div>
{note('Keep both parts: the cause (a natural gift, no effort) and the verdict (it doesn’t earn admiration).', 'tip')}"""),

